---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Slot libraries (grid, reels, pays, hold &amp; win)  - open-rgs"
  description="The @open-rgs/* slot libraries: shape-agnostic grids, weighted and Markov reel generation, mixture recipes, four pay evaluators, cascades and hold-and-win. Three signatures carry the whole system."
>
  <main>
    <h1>Slot libraries</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      Thirteen small packages that compose into a slot. They are ordinary
      TypeScript libraries your math file imports  - there is no plugin system,
      because TS math can simply <code>import</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 data-section="§ 1">Three signatures</h2>

    <p>
      Every piece in the stack is a pure function of a stream of floats. Nothing
      owns randomness; everything receives it.
    </p>

    <pre><code>{`Generator<S> = (next) => Grid<S>              // makes a board
Selector<S>  = (grid, next) => Pos[]          // picks cells
Effect       = (grid, pos, next) => Grid      // changes a board`}</code></pre>

    <p>
      <code>next</code> is always <code>host.rng_next</code>. That uniformity is
      not tidiness for its own sake  - it is what makes replay work. Because no
      piece can reach an ambient generator, a round is determined entirely by
      <code>(seed, carry)</code>, and <code>loadTsMath</code>'s
      <code>rngMode: "seed-expand"</code> can hand you one number that
      reconstructs it exactly.
    </p>

    <h2 data-section="§ 2">The layers</h2>

    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Packages</th><th>What it settles</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>substrate</td>
          <td><code>grid</code> <code>weights</code> <code>selectors</code></td>
          <td>Shape (ragged-first), exact weighted sampling, and "which cells?"</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>generation</td>
          <td><code>fill</code> <code>markov</code> <code>recipes</code></td>
          <td>Frequency, then stacking, then a mixture of named boards</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>evaluation</td>
          <td><code>paytable</code> <code>pay-lines</code> <code>pay-ways</code> <code>pay-anywhere</code> <code>pay-cluster</code></td>
          <td>Turning a board into a multiplier</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>features</td>
          <td><code>cascade</code> <code>holdwin</code></td>
          <td>Tumbles and respin cycles</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 data-section="§ 3">Four rules worth watching</h2>

    <p>
      Each of these is easy to state and easy to implement wrongly, and each
      costs real money when it is wrong.
    </p>

    <div class="anim">
      <span class="anim-head">Cascade &middot; clear, fall, refill</span>
      <div class="stage">
        <div>
          <div class="casc">
            <div class="sq a s0"></div>
            <div class="sq b s1"></div>
            <div class="sq c s2"></div>
            <div class="sq a s3"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="cap">one column</div>
        </div>
        <p>
          The winner flashes, clears, and everything above falls by exactly one
          cell before a fresh symbol drops in. Gravity is
          <strong>per column</strong>  - a short reel drops a shorter distance,
          and treating the board as a rectangle produces a shuffle rather than a
          fall.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="anim">
      <span class="anim-head">Stickiness changes clumping, not frequency</span>
      <div class="stage">
        <div>
          <div class="col roll loose">
            <div class="sq"></div><div class="sq"></div><div class="sq"></div>
            <div class="sq"></div><div class="sq"></div><div class="sq"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="cap">s = 0</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="col roll tight">
            <div class="sq"></div><div class="sq"></div><div class="sq"></div>
            <div class="sq"></div><div class="sq"></div><div class="sq"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="cap">s = 0.8</div>
        </div>
        <p>
          Both columns draw the same symbols at the same rates; only the runs
          differ. With <code>sticky(base, s)</code> the chain's stationary
          distribution is exactly <code>base</code> for every <code>s &lt; 1</code>,
          so this knob is pure feel and cannot move your RTP. That is the
          property a reel strip cannot offer.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="anim">
      <span class="anim-head">One wild, two clusters</span>
      <div class="stage">
        <div>
          <div class="wgrid">
            <div class="sq a lft"></div><div class="sq a lft"></div><div class="sq b rgt"></div>
            <div class="sq a lft"></div><div class="sq w both"></div><div class="sq b rgt"></div>
            <div class="sq c"></div><div class="sq b rgt"></div><div class="sq b rgt"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="cap">outlined wild sits in both</div>
        </div>
        <p>
          The centre wild belongs to both clusters, so the flood fill runs
          <strong>per symbol</strong> and consumes only real matches. Consume the
          wild for the first cluster and the second silently shrinks by one, with
          nothing on the board looking wrong.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="anim">
      <span class="anim-head">Hold &amp; win &middot; a landing RESETS the counter</span>
      <div class="stage">
        <div>
          <div class="meter">
            <div class="sq a"></div><div class="sq a m2"></div><div class="sq a m3"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="cap">respins left</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="sq b landing"></div>
          <div class="cap">new coin</div>
        </div>
        <p>
          Respins burn down, then a coin lands and the counter snaps back to
          full. That rule is what makes the feature feel alive  - and why a cycle
          has <strong>no natural end</strong>. A max-win cap is not optional in
          this genre; it is what makes the round finite.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 data-section="§ 4">A whole game</h2>

    <pre><code>{`import type { MathHost, SimpleMath } from "@open-rgs/contract";
import { rect } from "@open-rgs/grid";
import { stackyFill } from "@open-rgs/markov";
import { recipes, place, stack } from "@open-rgs/recipes";
import { randomN, cols } from "@open-rgs/selectors";
import { paytable, totalMultiplier } from "@open-rgs/paytable";
import { evalLines, rowLines } from "@open-rgs/pay-lines";

const SHAPE = rect(5, 3);
const BASE  = { LOW: 55, MID: 25, HIGH: 12, WILD: 5, SC: 3 };
const reels = stackyFill(SHAPE, BASE, 0.35);   // 0.35 = stackiness
const LINES = rowLines(5, 3);
const roles = { wilds: ["WILD"], scatters: ["SC"] };
const PAY   = paytable({ HIGH: { 3: 10, 4: 50, 5: 200 }, LOW: { 3: 2, 4: 5, 5: 20 } });

// Every board comes from exactly one named recipe, at a declared rate.
const board = recipes([
  { name: "base",        p: 0.94, draw: reels },
  { name: "two-scatter", p: 0.05, draw: place(2, "SC", randomN(2, cols([0, 1])), reels) },
  { name: "high-stack",  p: 0.01, draw: stack("HIGH", 2, reels) },
]);

export default (host: MathHost): SimpleMath => ({
  kind: "simple", name: "demo", version: "1.0.0", rtp: 0.96,
  play: () => {
    const { grid, recipe } = board(host.rng_next);
    const wins = evalLines(grid, LINES, PAY, { roles });
    const mult = totalMultiplier(wins);
    return {
      multiplier: mult,
      ops: [{ kind: "spin", grid: grid.cells, recipe }],
      type: mult > 0 ? "win" : "loss",
    };
  },
});`}</code></pre>

    <p class="note">
      Note the shape: it default-exports a <strong>factory</strong> taking the
      host. That is the RNG seam  - the math has no other source of randomness,
      and <code>loadTsMath</code> rejects a bare object for exactly that reason.
    </p>

    <h2 data-section="§ 5">Extending it</h2>

    <p>
      There is no plugin system to learn. Every extension point is one of the
      three signatures above, so extending means writing an ordinary function.
    </p>

    <pre><code>{`// A generator is (next) => Grid. Nothing to register.
const mirrored = (next) => {
  const left = reels(next);
  return fromColumns([...toColumns(left), ...toColumns(left).reverse()]);
};

// A selector is (grid, next) => Pos[]. Compose with the built-ins.
const topRow      = (grid, next) => rows([0])(grid, next);
const freeTopRow  = except(topRow, holding("WILD"));

// "Collect only from the same column" is a SELECTOR, not a new engine.
const sameColumn  = (src) => (grid, next) =>
  coinCells()(grid, next).filter((p) => p.col === src.col);
const columnCollector = (src) => collector(sameColumn(src), JACKPOTS);

// A new pay rule returns Win[] and concatenates with every other evaluator.
totalMultiplier([...evalLines(...), ...evalDiagonals(...)]);`}</code></pre>

    <p class="warn">
      <strong>The one rule for anything you add:</strong> take <code>next</code>,
      never make it. A function that reaches for <code>Math.random()</code> looks
      correct, passes every test you would think to write, and silently destroys
      seed reproducibility  - which voids any RTP measured from a replay.
    </p>

    <h2 data-section="§ 6">Package surface</h2>

    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>Reach for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>grid</code></td><td>rect &middot; makeGrid &middot; fromColumns &middot; at &middot; positionsWhere &middot; countOf &middot; withAt &middot; neighbours</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>weights</code></td><td>sampler &middot; pickDistinct &middot; probabilityOf &middot; distribution</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>selectors</code></td><td>all &middot; cols &middot; rows &middot; where &middot; holding &middot; randomN &middot; upTo &middot; union &middot; except &middot; not</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>fill</code></td><td>fillWeights &middot; fillWeightsPerColumn &middot; constant &middot; expectedCount</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>markov</code></td><td>stackyFill &middot; sticky &middot; transitions &middot; stationary &middot; meanRunLength</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>recipes</code></td><td>recipes &middot; place &middot; placeAt &middot; placeDrawn &middot; stack &middot; fixed</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>paytable</code></td><td>paytable &middot; bands &middot; substitutes &middot; isWild &middot; isScatter &middot; totalMultiplier</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>pay-lines</code></td><td>evalLine &middot; evalLines &middot; rowLines</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>pay-ways</code></td><td>evalWay &middot; evalWays &middot; totalWays</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>pay-anywhere</code></td><td>evalAnywhere &middot; evalScatters &middot; triggersOn &middot; shortOfTrigger</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>pay-cluster</code></td><td>clustersOf &middot; evalClusters &middot; evalAllClusters &middot; largestCluster</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>cascade</code></td><td>runCascade &middot; tumble &middot; clear &middot; collapse &middot; refill</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>holdwin</code></td><td>triggers &middot; beginRespins &middot; stepRespins &middot; settleRespins &middot; collector &middot; payer &middot; multiplier &middot; upgrader &middot; spawner</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Animatics: square divs, no rounded corners, CSS keyframes only. */
  .anim {
    border: 1px solid var(--fg-mute, #888);
    padding: 1.4rem;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1rem;
  }
  .anim-head {
    font-family: var(--mono, monospace);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--fg-mute, #888);
  }
  .stage { display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 1.9rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .stage p { flex: 1; min-width: 15rem; margin: 0; }
  .col { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 3px; }
  .cap {
    font-family: var(--mono, monospace);
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    color: var(--fg-mute, #888);
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
  }
  .sq {
    width: 26px; height: 26px;
    background: var(--bg-soft, #eee);
    border: 1px solid var(--fg-mute, #888);
    border-radius: 0;
  }
  .sq.a { background: #0E6B62; border-color: #0E6B62; }
  .sq.b { background: #8A5A15; border-color: #8A5A15; }
  .sq.c { background: #4A6570; border-color: #4A6570; }
  .sq.w { background: transparent; border: 2px solid #0E6B62; }

  .casc { position: relative; height: 116px; width: 26px; overflow: hidden; }
  .casc .sq { position: absolute; left: 0; }
  @keyframes fallOne {
    0%, 22%   { transform: translateY(0); }
    30%, 100% { transform: translateY(29px); }
  }
  @keyframes clearOne {
    0%, 18%  { opacity: 1; outline: 0 solid #0E6B62; }
    22%      { opacity: 1; outline: 3px solid #0E6B62; outline-offset: -3px; }
    28%, 92% { opacity: 0; outline: 0 solid #0E6B62; }
    100%     { opacity: 0; }
  }
  @keyframes dropIn {
    0%, 26%   { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(-29px); }
    36%, 100% { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
  }
  .casc .s0 { top: 0;    animation: dropIn 3.2s infinite ease-out; }
  .casc .s1 { top: 29px; animation: fallOne 3.2s infinite ease-in; }
  .casc .s2 { top: 58px; animation: fallOne 3.2s infinite ease-in; }
  .casc .s3 { top: 87px; animation: clearOne 3.2s infinite; }

  .roll .sq { animation: rollA 4s infinite steps(1, end); }
  @keyframes rollA { 0%{background:#0E6B62;border-color:#0E6B62}25%{background:#8A5A15;border-color:#8A5A15}50%{background:#4A6570;border-color:#4A6570}75%{background:#0E6B62;border-color:#0E6B62} }
  @keyframes rollB { 0%{background:#8A5A15;border-color:#8A5A15}25%{background:#0E6B62;border-color:#0E6B62}50%{background:#0E6B62;border-color:#0E6B62}75%{background:#4A6570;border-color:#4A6570} }
  @keyframes rollC { 0%{background:#4A6570;border-color:#4A6570}25%{background:#4A6570;border-color:#4A6570}50%{background:#8A5A15;border-color:#8A5A15}75%{background:#8A5A15;border-color:#8A5A15} }
  .loose .sq:nth-child(1){animation-name:rollA}.loose .sq:nth-child(2){animation-name:rollC}
  .loose .sq:nth-child(3){animation-name:rollB}.loose .sq:nth-child(4){animation-name:rollA}
  .loose .sq:nth-child(5){animation-name:rollC}.loose .sq:nth-child(6){animation-name:rollB}
  .tight .sq:nth-child(1){animation-name:rollA}.tight .sq:nth-child(2){animation-name:rollA}
  .tight .sq:nth-child(3){animation-name:rollA}.tight .sq:nth-child(4){animation-name:rollB}
  .tight .sq:nth-child(5){animation-name:rollB}.tight .sq:nth-child(6){animation-name:rollC}

  .wgrid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 26px); gap: 3px; }
  @keyframes ringL { 0%,45%{outline:3px solid #0E6B62;outline-offset:-3px}50%,100%{outline:0 solid transparent} }
  @keyframes ringR { 0%,45%{outline:0 solid transparent}50%,100%{outline:3px solid #8A5A15;outline-offset:-3px} }
  .wgrid .lft  { animation: ringL 3s infinite; }
  .wgrid .rgt  { animation: ringR 3s infinite; }
  .wgrid .both { animation: ringL 3s infinite, ringR 3s infinite; }

  .meter { display: flex; gap: 3px; }
  @keyframes burn3 { 0%,24%{opacity:1}25%,66%{opacity:.15}67%,100%{opacity:1} }
  @keyframes burn2 { 0%,49%{opacity:1}50%,66%{opacity:.15}67%,100%{opacity:1} }
  @keyframes coinLand { 0%,60%{opacity:0;transform:translateY(-10px)}67%,100%{opacity:1;transform:translateY(0)} }
  .meter .m3 { animation: burn3 4s infinite steps(1, end); }
  .meter .m2 { animation: burn2 4s infinite steps(1, end); }
  .landing   { animation: coinLand 4s infinite; }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .casc .sq, .roll .sq, .wgrid div, .meter div, .landing {
      animation: none !important;
    }
    .casc .s3 { opacity: 0.3; }
  }
</style>
